<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">What bacteria most commonly cause mouth infections?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">The most common oral bacterial infections involve Streptococcus and Staphylococcus species, as well as anaerobic bacteria like Prevotella, Fusobacterium, and Peptostreptococcus. Mixed infections (aerobic + anaerobic) are frequent in dental abscesses. Culture testing at CARE Hospitals identifies the specific organisms so our surgeons can prescribe the most effective antibiotic.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">Can bacteria from a mouth infection enter my bloodstream?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">Yes — oral bacteria regularly enter the bloodstream during dental procedures and can cause bacteremia. In patients with certain heart conditions or prosthetic joints, this can lead to serious complications like infective endocarditis. Our surgeons assess these risks during treatment planning.</div>
</details>
<details class="group bg-surface-container-lowest border border-surface-variant rounded-lg [&_summary::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
<summary class="flex items-center justify-between p-6 cursor-pointer font-semibold text-primary">How long does a bacterial mouth infection take to heal?<span class="material-symbols-outlined transition duration-300 group-open:-rotate-180">expand_more</span></summary>
<div class="px-6 pb-6 text-on-surface-variant text-sm">With appropriate antibiotic treatment, most oral bacterial infections improve within 48–72 hours and resolve in 7–14 days. Surgical drainage of abscesses typically gives pain relief within hours. Recurrence is prevented only when the source tooth or tissue is also treated — not just with antibiotics alone.</div>
</details>
